// oracle_enum_println.sf — IO.println(enum_value) must print the variant, not a
// reinterpretation of its bit pattern.
//
// Found by the differential oracle on its first real run: test/pass/enums.sf and
// test/pass/generics.sf are BOTH printing lines like `7.29112e-304` and
// `5.21502e-310` today, on native, at -O0 and -O2 alike, and both are counted as
// passing tests. They have no assertions and no .expected file, so nothing in
// tools/run_tests.sh can see it.
//
// MECHANISM. A fieldless enum variant is represented as the immediate
// `tag << 56`. Codegen's IO.println path passes that value straight through:
//
//     %t2 = shl i64 0, 56
//     %t3 = call i64 @__io_println(i64 %t2)
//
// with no `Color__to_string` call and no int tag applied. `__io_println` inspects
// the NaN-box tag bits, finds none of TAG_PTR/TAG_INT/TAG_SPEC set, and correctly
// concludes the value is an unboxed double — so it formats the raw bit pattern as
// a float. `0 << 56` is 0 and prints "0"; `1 << 56` is 0x0100000000000000, a
// subnormal double, and prints "7.29112e-304".
//
// The interpolation path is CORRECT for the same value, because the lexer's
// `"${e}"` desugaring inserts an explicit `.to_string()`:
//
//     %t7 = call i64 @Color__to_string(i64 %t6)
//
// So `IO.println("${Color.Red}")` prints "Red" while `IO.println(Color.Red)`
// prints "0". Two code paths, one right and one wrong, for the same value — which
// is the M5 "duplicated logic that drifts" mechanism from
// docs/design/compiler-rewrite.md, and the same shape as BUGS #77 (branching on a
// bool was right while printing it was wrong).
//
// This is closely related to BUGS #102 but is NOT the same bug. #102 was inside
// the generated `to_string()` — it decoded a one-field enum's heap pointer as a
// `tag << 56` immediate. This one never CALLS to_string() at all. #102's fix
// makes to_string() correct, which is why the interpolation path above works; the
// direct-println path bypasses it and is still wrong.
//
// Expected output is in the sibling .expected file and records CORRECT behaviour,
// so this test fails until the println path is fixed.

enum Color { Red, Green, Blue }

// Fieldless variants, printed directly. This is the broken path.
IO.println(Color.Red)
IO.println(Color.Green)
IO.println(Color.Blue)

// The same three values through interpolation. This path works today, and having
// both here means a regression in EITHER direction is visible in one test — a
// "fix" that broke interpolation to match println would not look like a pass.
IO.println("${Color.Red}")
IO.println("${Color.Green}")
IO.println("${Color.Blue}")

// And through an explicit .to_string(), the third path.
IO.println(Color.Red.to_string())
IO.println(Color.Blue.to_string())

// Via a variable, so the value has round-tripped through a global slot rather
// than being consumed straight from the constructor.
var c = Color.Green
IO.println(c)
IO.println("${c}")
IO.println(c.to_string())

// Payload-carrying variants, direct println. Kept distinct from the fieldless
// case because their representation is a heap pointer rather than an immediate,
// so they fail differently (or not at all) and the difference is diagnostic.
enum Shape {
    Circle(r: Float),
    Square(side: Int),
    Named(name: String)
}

IO.println(Shape.Circle(2.5))
IO.println(Shape.Square(4))
IO.println(Shape.Named("box"))
IO.println("${Shape.Circle(2.5)}")
IO.println("${Shape.Square(4)}")
IO.println("${Shape.Named("box")}")

// A fieldless variant of an enum that ALSO has payload variants. The tag is still
// an immediate, but the enum's to_string() has to handle both shapes, so this is
// the case where a partial fix is most likely to land wrong.
enum Mixed {
    Nothing,
    Just(v: Int)
}

IO.println(Mixed.Nothing)
IO.println(Mixed.Just(7))
IO.println("${Mixed.Nothing}")
IO.println("${Mixed.Just(7)}")

// Inside a list, which goes through the list element formatter rather than
// __io_println directly — a fourth path, and it is broken the SAME way: today
// this prints `[0, 4.77831e-299]`. So the working path is specifically the one
// that goes through an explicit `.to_string()` call, and every path that formats
// an enum value without one is wrong. That narrows the fix to "apply the enum's
// to_string() wherever an enum value reaches a formatter", rather than to any one
// call site.
IO.println([Color.Red, Color.Blue])
IO.println([Mixed.Nothing, Mixed.Just(1)])
